            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Forty-four medical students prepared for two pathology cases. For one, they used a language model aligned with their curriculum; for the other, conventional resources. AI-supported preparation and discussion received higher scores. Yet the later test showed no statistically significant difference. This <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42873617/">small pilot study</a> raises a substantial educational question: how much of the better preparation stayed in the learner's mind?</p>
<p>Imagine a student called Nela at the Javor teaching clinic. Both the name and the clinic are fictional; this is not an account of an actual event. Nela receives a fictional kidney disease case. An assistant offers a fluent explanation, and she feels she understands it. The teacher then changes one important detail and asks her to justify her decision without help. Only then can the teacher see which relationships she understands independently.</p>
<p>That moment interests me. The screen goes dark. The learner is left with a problem and their own judgment. Can they explain why they chose a particular approach? Will they notice a detail that does not fit the polished answer?</p>
<p>A <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42085678/">review of 66 randomized trials involving 4,911 participants</a> supports similar caution. Some educational outcomes were favorable, but certainty of evidence was low to very low. None of the included trials assessed changes in behavior in practice or final care outcomes. A well-rated learning tool does not by itself tell us how the patient will fare.</p>
<p>There are nevertheless reasons to try these tools. In a <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42902927/">randomized trial with 51 participants</a>, brief training with a virtual patient improved subsequent communication with a person trained to portray a patient more than reading materials did. Assessment took place on the same day. A <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42987316/">year-long study with 50 students</a> found no statistically significant advantage for another simulator over usual teaching. Results from one tool cannot be credited to every other tool.</p>
<p>I would therefore try a three-step learning sequence. First, students write their own solution, including their uncertainties. Next, the simulator guides them with questions, and a teacher discusses the errors with them. Finally, perhaps a week later, they tackle a new case without AI. Both the decision and its reasoning are assessed. This is a proposal for local evaluation, not a proven method.</p>
<p>I would also add an exercise with a deliberately flawed answer prepared in advance by the teacher. Students must find the weakness and correct it using a verified source. This allows us to observe whether someone can challenge persuasive prose. Fictional cases also remove the need to enter real health records into an external system.</p>
<p>In nephrology, possible uses include conversations about treatment options and explanations of laboratory findings. I discuss the evidence and its limits in more detail in a <a href="https://nefro.polascin.net/article.php?slug=umela-inteligencia-nefrologicke-vzdelavanie-dokazy-hranice">professional review in Slovak</a>. Here, my concern is a principle that also applies outside medicine: a learning aid should gradually enable people to do more for themselves.</p>
<p>I would judge a good educational AI partly by how well its users manage after switching it off.</p>
<p><em>This is a reflection on education. The illustrative situation is fictional; the proposed exercises are not a clinically or educationally validated program, and this text is not treatment advice.</em></p>

